as one of the most seismic moments in  american history was unfolding the  president was in the most unlikely of  settings  moments before entering that florida  classroom he'd been told a small plane  had hit the first tower  the president the principal and i all  had the same reaction oh what a horrible  accident the pilot must have had a heart  attack or something for his chief of  staff  the picture soon changed captain lowe  came back up to me and said oh my god  another plane hit the other tower at the  world trade center  and i knew that this was not a  coincidence  it wasn't an accident  and so i decided to pass on two facts  and make one editorial comment to the  president and to do nothing to invite a  dialogue with them i thought about what  i would say i opened the door to the  room came in  that's when i walked up to the president  and i leaned over and i whispered into  his right ear a second plane  hit the second tower  america is under attack  and i looked at the president i could  still see him facing forward  with his head bobbing up and down  i honestly believe he was thinking about  the burden that he accepted when he took  the oath of office  i like to say that's when he became the  president  and i was very impressed that he did  nothing to introduce fear to those  second graders he did nothing to  demonstrate fear to the media that would  have satisfied the terrorists all around  the world  charity wallace was at the white house  i remember just this cacophony of sound  of people yelling and screaming and  running in the heels on that tile  and secret service were running and  that's really what scared me because i'd  never seen secret service run before and  as we were running towards the gate  the agent looked at all the women and  said take your shoes off and run for  your life and i  physically stopped because  it was such a jarring and terrifying  thing to hear and i just said  where are we supposed to go and he said  get as far away from here as you can  what would have seemed unthinkable 20  years ago now after the withdrawal from  afghanistan has become a reality  what's happened in afghanistan has just  made mrs bush really sad and sick  honestly for the women we've talked a  lot about that  um there they will be commemorating um  september 11th this 20th anniversary  and  i think that again they reflect  like i do on the courage and bravery on  the sacrifice of the men and women in  uniform and all of that i do think that  there is a sense that people have  forgotten  in a way  that day and everything that happened  that day  the fight for freedom  that united our country at the time  do you fear that there's a danger that  people forget  i've always felt that it was a slight  danger i think that it's been  exacerbated by what happened in  afghanistan over the last month i am  haunted by the images of 9 11 and i want  other people to feel the emotion out of  respect i remember september 11 2001  every day i feel more convicted to make  sure young people understand what  happened  so  it's for me it's not 20 years ago for me  it's yesterday  a moment in history this weekend they  will remember greg milam sky news new  york
 

